---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy policy — Milton"
  description="Milton is operated by Zone of Danger, LLC (“we”)."
  legal
>
  <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
  <p class="updated">
    Last updated: <time datetime="2026-11-08">November 8, 2026</time>
  </p>
  <p>
    Milton is operated by Zone of Danger, LLC (“we”). Milton keeps the writing
    you approved, word for word. It never rewrites a rule or a sample, and it
    never reads your inbox. The details follow.
  </p>

  <h2>What Milton stores</h2>
  <p style="text-wrap:pretty">
    Your name and email address when you sign up or sign in, and the identifiers
    of Google or GitHub accounts you use to sign in or link; password hashes if
    you use a password; sign-in sessions, email verification and password reset
    records, AI permissions and access tokens, and security rate-limit records;
    the writing rules you ask your AIs to save; the samples of your writing you
    approve for saving, stored exactly as approved, including any names they
    contain; each rule and sample you saved, with its versions and which AI
    saved it; request receipts used to prevent duplicate saves; internal records
    of accepted changes, including the exact submitted rules, edits or samples,
    authorship and affected versions; connected AIs and the AIs you disconnect;
    counts of tool calls you make each day; and your billing record: your Stripe
    customer and subscription IDs, the subscription’s status, its trial and
    billing period dates, and which trial reminders have been sent.
  </p>

  <h2>How we use it</h2>
  <p>
    Only to run Milton: to sign you in, verify your email, help you recover and
    secure your account, store your rules, retain their internal history,
    prepare the cover sheet and reports for the AIs you connect, and run your
    trial and subscription. Accepted-change records remain with your internal
    history until account deletion. Your data is encrypted at rest by our
    database provider. We do not sell your data, show ads, or use your rules or
    samples to train AI models.
  </p>
  <p>
    Email/password sign-in requires a verified email address. If you use a
    password, Milton’s sign-in service processes it and stores a salted password
    hash using Better Auth’s scrypt hashing, rather than the password itself.
    Verification and password reset emails go through Resend, which receives
    your email address and the message, including its verification or reset
    link.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can see your rules and samples</h2>
  <p>
    You and the AIs you connect receive your current cover sheet and reports,
    samples included. Once an AI has read them, its handling of that content is
    governed by the terms and privacy policy of that AI’s provider, not ours.
    Rules and samples are stored exactly as your AI sends them; Ordinary saves
    and reads do not send them to an AI model. If you explicitly opt into the
    private guide pilot, the background processing described below sends
    approved samples to Anthropic. Milton does not collect your AI
    conversations. The service providers below process data to operate Milton.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can disconnect an AI at any time from Connected AIs in the Milton app.
    Milton blocks further access, even with an existing authorization. This does
    not erase content the AI already received. To use it again, reconnect it and
    approve access.
  </p>

  <h2>Samples</h2>
  <p style="text-wrap:pretty">
    A sample is saved only after you say yes to a specific piece of writing, and
    it is stored exactly as you approved it. Milton does not remove names or
    other details from it; you decide what to save. Saving a new sample keeps
    your existing samples unless you ask to replace or remove them. You can ask
    your AI to replace or remove a sample. Removed or replaced samples leave
    future reports and current downloads, but remain in your internal history
    until account deletion. This does not erase copies an AI already received.
  </p>

  <h2>Optional derived guides</h2>
  <p>
    Guides are off by default. For eligible accounts that explicitly opt in,
    Milton sends every active approved sample in a report, with its approved
    classification and context, to Anthropic to infer writing tendencies. Rules,
    retired sample revisions and earlier guides are excluded. The resulting
    guide is an interpretation, shown to you and the AIs you connect; your
    approved writing stays unchanged. A stale guide can still reflect a sample
    that was corrected or removed until its update succeeds. Removing all active
    samples clears the guide immediately. Guides are excluded from your
    download.
  </p>
  <p>
    Anthropic’s standard API policy deletes inputs and outputs within 30 days,
    with exceptions for policy enforcement, legal requirements and contractual
    terms. This pilot does not promise zero data retention. See{" "}
    <a href="https://privacy.claude.com/en/articles/7996866-how-long-do-you-store-my-organization-s-data">
      Anthropic’s retention policy
    </a>
    . Disabling participation hides guides on future reads; it cannot retract
    copies already received by an AI. Completed guides may remain stored while
    disabled, until account deletion. Milton keeps one current guide per report,
    without a guide history archive.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it is kept</h2>
  <p>
    The database runs on Neon (United States). Milton operates its own sign-in
    service using Better Auth at auth.meetmilton.com on Google Cloud Run. Resend
    delivers verification and password reset emails. The public website runs on
    Vercel; the app and MCP server run on Google Cloud Run (United States). The
    public website uses Vercel Web Analytics, which sets no cookies: it records
    each page view’s address and query parameters, referrer, time, browser,
    operating system, device type and location down to city, and counts visitors
    with a daily-reset hash of the request instead of tying records to a person
    or IP address. DNS is on Cloudflare. Google and GitHub also handle your
    sign-in with them. Stripe processes subscriptions and payments. Earlier
    managed sign-in records remain separately in Neon after the old sign-in
    service is disabled. Its database structure, data and backups require their
    own cleanup; deleting a new sign-in does not remove that earlier copy.
  </p>

  <p>
    Verification and reset messages wait in an encrypted delivery queue with the
    original destination and secret link. Delivery stops at the earlier of the
    link’s expiry and 15 minutes after acceptance. Marking a message as sent,
    failed or cancelled clears its encrypted contents. Delivery processing and
    cleanup remove expired contents in small batches; delayed or disabled
    cleanup can retain them longer. Message receipts include original sign-in
    identifiers but no recipient or link and become eligible for removal 24
    hours after acceptance. Actual removal depends on that cleanup. Resend’s
    copies follow its own retention.
  </p>

  <h2>Billing</h2>
  <p>
    Milton creates a Stripe customer for you when your free trial starts, and
    starts your trial subscription at Stripe with no card. If you had no trial,
    the customer is created when you first open billing. Milton gives Stripe
    your Milton account ID and, if Milton has it at that moment, your sign-in
    email address. Each time you open billing from the Milton app, Milton sends
    Stripe your current sign-in email address for that customer.
  </p>
  <p>
    You enter card and other payment details on Stripe’s pages, not Milton’s.
    They go to Stripe, and Milton does not store them.
  </p>

  <h2>Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    The app and sign-in service use separate cookies, each limited to its own
    hostname, to keep you signed in and protect sign-in and account actions.
    Signing out ends browser access; AIs with offline access stay connected.
    Changing or resetting your password, or unlinking a sign-in method,
    invalidates earlier browser sessions and AI authorizations. AIs must
    reconnect with your consent to regain access.
  </p>

  <h2>Deleting your account</h2>
  <p>
    Choose Delete account in the Milton app. Deletion blocks account and AI
    access. If you have a Stripe customer, deletion deletes it at Stripe first,
    which ends any subscription immediately, with no refund. Milton then removes
    your rules, samples, derived guides and pending guide work, internal memos
    and their history, your cover sheet and reports, request receipts,
    accepted-change records, usage counts and billing record, and the app’s
    sign-in sessions and credentials. When you delete your account, Milton’s
    sign-in service also deletes your sign-in profile, password hashes, linked
    sign-in methods, sessions, and every AI connection you approved. An AI
    registration that only you approved is deleted; one that another account
    also approved stays, with its name and contact details erased. We keep only
    a one-way fingerprint of each deleted or erased registration’s ID, so its
    details stay erased if it registers again. Any registration approved before
    November 8, 2026 that can no longer be linked to an account remains as
    registered. If account cleanup is incomplete, the app offers a retry;
    deletion is confirmed only when that cleanup finishes. Unused password reset
    links stop working when account security changes or deletion starts, as well
    as when they expire. Verification links expire after one hour; remaining
    verification records become eligible for cleanup one day after expiry and
    can remain until cleanup reaches them. The limited records below remain.
  </p>
  <p>These records remain after deletion:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Records of original account deletions and AI disconnections, including
      original account, sign-in and AI identifiers and connection references, so
      retries cannot affect a later account or connection. These contain no
      writing or credentials.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keyed fingerprints of verified email addresses and stable Google or GitHub
      account identifiers, with the internal account that first claimed them, to
      prevent another free trial. This trial-eligibility record does not store
      raw email addresses or provider identifiers and is not used to merge
      writing accounts.
    </li>
    <li>
      Limited verified records linking earlier sign-in identities to Milton
      account IDs, to preserve account and deletion history, and a check that
      trial fingerprints remain consistent.
    </li>
    <li>
      Content-free guide spending records (amount, time and a one-way account
      fingerprint), so deletion cannot reset pilot spending limits.
    </li>
    <li>Stripe keeps past invoices and payment records.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These limited identity and security records are kept without a fixed expiry
    for those purposes. They do not restore deleted writing or authorize access.
    Expired sign-in sessions, verification and reset records, and expired AI
    tokens can remain until periodic cleanup; records still needed for active
    offline access or unfinished cleanup remain until that work can finish.
  </p>
  <p>
    Copies in database backups are not erased immediately; they are removed when
    those backups expire or are deleted. You can also email{" "}
    <a href="mailto:support@meetmilton.com">support@meetmilton.com</a> and we
    will delete your account for you.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>We will update the date above when this policy changes.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="mailto:support@meetmilton.com">support@meetmilton.com</a>.
  </p>
</SiteLayout>
